00:14:04.515 --> 00:14:07.584
We want to check to make sure
everything's balanced, but the one

00:14:07.584 --> 00:14:11.814
thing is that you might notice is that,
well, we have a liquid in the problem.

00:14:12.215 --> 00:14:17.194
So what that means is we're not going
to end up including it in our KEQ.

00:14:17.665 --> 00:14:19.795
So we'll still do products over reactants.

00:14:19.805 --> 00:14:24.905
So we have CL2, and then we want
to put that all over the reactants,

00:14:25.355 --> 00:14:27.495
which is going to be PCL5.

00:14:27.974 --> 00:14:31.504
So that will be down in
the denominator, so PCL5.

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:35.770
And being that that does not
have a power, this would be the

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:37.390
correct equilibrium expression.

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:44.219
Now for the next one, we have FeO,
that's a solid, plus CO gas, and FeS.

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:48.449
So what we're going to be doing here
is once again checking all the phases.

00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:53.185
So you see that we have a solid, and
then another solid, so we're not going

00:14:53.185 --> 00:14:55.100
to include those, only the gases.

00:14:55.660 --> 00:14:58.290
So when we write this, we
do products over reactants.

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:04.050
So we have CO2 to the
power one all over CO.

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:06.649
And you're going to see like,
Oh, these are pretty easy, right?

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:08.630
It's all about products or reactants.

00:15:08.689 --> 00:15:12.380
Say that to yourself, products over
reactants, products over reactants.

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:16.339
That's something that's going to kind of
stay home for a lot of different points

00:15:16.339 --> 00:15:20.910
in chemistry, especially if you have to
take gen chem one or gen chem two as a,

00:15:21.119 --> 00:15:22.900
as either chemistry or biology major.

00:15:23.175 --> 00:15:25.845
You're going to see that pop up in
different sections of the class.

00:15:26.595 --> 00:15:26.865
All right.

00:15:26.875 --> 00:15:29.505
Now the last one, what we have
is once again, we're looking

00:15:29.505 --> 00:15:30.865
for all the different phases.

00:15:31.205 --> 00:15:34.714
So one thing that we can see is
that, oh, we have a solid here, so

00:15:34.714 --> 00:15:36.644
we're not going to include that.

00:15:36.655 --> 00:15:39.607
So when we do products,
we have K and O three.

00:15:39.607 --> 00:15:45.575
And one thing that we can kind of notice
is that it's has a two coefficient.

00:15:45.575 --> 00:15:47.285
So we'll put a power two in front of it.

00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:55.710
Then we do our reactants, so we have
PbNO3, and that's going to be 2, that's

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:57.860
a subscript of 2, outside of brackets.

00:15:58.875 --> 00:16:03.135
And then we have our KCL, so
we'll put that in another color

00:16:03.135 --> 00:16:04.465
just to help emphasize it.

00:16:04.775 --> 00:16:09.975
And that's to a power of 2 because of
the coefficient on the left hand side.

00:16:10.484 --> 00:16:14.045
Okay, so we want to make sure that
we kind of follow along with all

00:16:14.045 --> 00:16:18.055
these, plugging them all in, remember
products over reactants, place your

00:16:18.055 --> 00:16:22.254
powers, um, so it's just putting your
coefficients as powers, and this is

00:16:22.295 --> 00:16:23.775
how we do these kinds of problems.

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:26.040
Let's go on to our next type of problem.

00:16:26.230 --> 00:16:29.800
So one of the very common problems is
you doing Le Chatelier's principle.

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:32.540
And this is something that usually
you have to memorize the different

00:16:32.549 --> 00:16:36.409
trends unless you're able to kind
of visualize how the reactions

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:38.119
shift forward and reverse.

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:41.389
If you're planning on being a chemist
or a biologist, I would recommend

00:16:41.389 --> 00:16:44.850
remembering the trends because it
does help you be able to predict and

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:47.380
understand how chemical reactions occur.

00:16:47.720 --> 00:16:51.760
But if you're just trying to get through
this course, then at least knowing them

00:16:51.770 --> 00:16:53.400
until you get to the end of your exam.

00:16:53.689 --> 00:16:56.829
Um, but a lot of them kind of
follow some common sense kind of

00:16:56.829 --> 00:16:58.899
things about how reactions work.

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:03.510
Um, the more you kind of realize of how it
relates to your actual world around you.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:06.780
So, one of the things is, well,
what direction would equilibrium

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:11.470
shift for the following changes
in this endothermic reaction?

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:14.810
Okay, so it's being told what
kind of reaction we have.

00:17:15.250 --> 00:17:18.500
So being that it says that
it's endothermic, one thing

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:21.150
that you have to remember is,
well, where do you place heat?

00:17:21.399 --> 00:17:23.700
Is heat being absorbed or is it released?

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:26.579
Endo is a positive value.

00:17:26.579 --> 00:17:28.810
It's something that is
being, is being absorbed.

00:17:29.175 --> 00:17:34.085
So, what you would do is, this is going
to be a reactant on the left hand side.

00:17:34.755 --> 00:17:39.504
If it was XO, then it would be written
as a product on the right hand side.

00:17:39.765 --> 00:17:42.494
This is, could affect how you
would list some of these different

00:17:42.504 --> 00:17:44.225
values and how they would change.

00:17:44.724 --> 00:17:49.235
So, the first one is, well, if carbon
monoxide is removed, what would occur?

00:17:49.635 --> 00:17:55.195
So, if I remove CO, well, which
direction is the reaction going to shift?

00:17:55.585 --> 00:17:59.475
Well, it's going to panic and realize
that, oh, all of this is missing.

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:06.209
So it's going to end up shifting towards
the right to create more CO as a result.

00:18:06.490 --> 00:18:11.070
So with this one, we will shift
towards the products as a result.

00:18:11.959 --> 00:18:16.419
If I go to the next one, which is
what if temperature is decreased, what

00:18:16.419 --> 00:18:18.709
would exactly happen in that result?

00:18:19.090 --> 00:18:23.080
Well temperature, the way that we treat
it, it's very similar to a concentration.

00:18:23.290 --> 00:18:25.660
If the reaction's getting
really, really cold, it's gonna

00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:26.740
be like, well, how do I what?

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:27.520
What do I do?

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:31.000
Well, if you get really cold in real
life, well, what's the first thing you do?

00:18:31.030 --> 00:18:31.600
You add?

00:18:31.605 --> 00:18:34.720
Either add more layers, turn
up the heat, you do something

00:18:34.810 --> 00:18:36.370
to warm yourself up, right?

00:18:36.370 --> 00:18:39.130
That common sense of what
do you do in everyday life.

00:18:39.490 --> 00:18:43.270
So if that were to happen, we
would shift the reaction in

00:18:43.270 --> 00:18:46.240
response to the reactant side.

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:48.010
So we will shift it the other way.

00:18:48.699 --> 00:18:55.129
So reactants, and we would try to heat
ourselves up by making more reactants.

00:18:55.780 --> 00:18:58.299
Now what if carbon was removed?

00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:02.470
So carbon, if you can see right
here on the, on the left hand

00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:04.849
side, notice how carbon is there.

00:19:05.189 --> 00:19:06.280
So what if I remove it?

00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:08.950
Do I follow the same trend I
did before, where if I take

00:19:08.950 --> 00:19:10.490
something away, I shift towards it.

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:13.300
But notice one thing next to it, right?

00:19:13.409 --> 00:19:14.929
It is a solid.

00:19:15.030 --> 00:19:15.560
Ooh.

00:19:15.999 --> 00:19:16.729
So what does that mean?

00:19:16.739 --> 00:19:19.989
Well, if you remember, how would
you write the K expression?

00:19:20.375 --> 00:19:23.645
Well, would you include a
solid in your final expression?

00:19:23.985 --> 00:19:26.004
And the one thing you can
remember is you don't.

00:19:26.304 --> 00:19:28.814
So would it have a change in this case?

00:19:29.125 --> 00:19:30.675
No, there would be no effect.

00:19:31.185 --> 00:19:34.905
So if you have a solid or a
liquid, it doesn't do anything.

00:19:35.044 --> 00:19:36.324
So that's one thing to remember, right?

00:19:36.324 --> 00:19:37.655
It's less work for us for later.

00:19:38.264 --> 00:19:38.525
All right.

00:19:38.565 --> 00:19:40.805
Now, what if pressure is increased?

00:19:40.805 --> 00:19:41.251
All right.

00:19:41.251 --> 00:19:43.865
When it comes to pressure, the
thing that we have to do is we got

00:19:43.865 --> 00:19:46.314
to look at the number of moles.

00:19:46.649 --> 00:19:48.409
Well, on the different
sides of the reaction.

00:19:48.889 --> 00:19:52.120
So if we want to increase pressure,
what it's going to do, it's going to

00:19:52.120 --> 00:19:54.679
shift away from more moles of gas.

00:19:55.060 --> 00:19:58.060
So one thing that we see is if you
look on the right hand side, we have

00:19:58.090 --> 00:20:04.969
three moles of gas that we have the
two moles of CO, one mole of CO2.

00:20:04.969 --> 00:20:06.039
So there's three total.

00:20:06.639 --> 00:20:10.120
On the left hand side, well,
there is zero moles of gas.

00:20:10.490 --> 00:20:13.469
So which way could it
go to relieve pressure?

00:20:13.719 --> 00:20:15.779
And that way, how do
you, where can you go?

00:20:15.789 --> 00:20:19.820
Where, where's it less crowded on the
left hand side, on the right hand side?

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:23.159
And one thing that you'll notice
is that, well, if I end up shifting

00:20:23.159 --> 00:20:27.980
this to the left, Then it's
going to be a lot less crowded.

00:20:28.220 --> 00:20:31.260
So we're going to be shifting
it towards the reactants if

00:20:31.260 --> 00:20:33.040
we increased the pressure.

00:20:33.579 --> 00:20:33.899
All right.

00:20:34.189 --> 00:20:36.329
Now that's how we look at
Le Chatelier's principle.

00:20:36.379 --> 00:20:38.229
Let's go take a look at our next example.

00:20:38.499 --> 00:20:40.269
Consider the following example.

00:20:40.329 --> 00:20:44.709
So if I have two SO2 and it's reacting
with oxygen gas to make sulfur trioxide

00:20:45.939 --> 00:20:48.580
and I have a K that's equal to 680.

00:20:49.260 --> 00:20:53.669
Now from that, if at equilibrium
we have the concentration of SO2.

00:20:53.955 --> 00:20:58.645
Which is point 300 molar
oxygen, which is 0.150 molar.

00:20:59.215 --> 00:21:02.125
And I ask, what is the
concentration of SO three?

00:21:02.125 --> 00:21:03.145
How would you do that?

00:21:03.635 --> 00:21:07.055
Well, for this particular problem,
if you need to find a concentration

00:21:07.055 --> 00:21:08.735
of an individual species.

00:21:09.095 --> 00:21:11.195
You need to rearrange the problem.

00:21:11.225 --> 00:21:15.145
You need to re, you need to create your
K expression and then rearrange it.

00:21:15.495 --> 00:21:19.005
So the first thing is, if I want to set
up my expression, well, you know that

00:21:19.005 --> 00:21:24.435
680 is equal to K and then it's gonna be
equal to the products over the reactants.

00:21:24.615 --> 00:21:30.755
So we have SO three, which is what we
want, and all of that is gonna be over

00:21:30.875 --> 00:21:33.605
SO two, which is to the second power.

00:21:34.245 --> 00:21:36.095
And then also oxygen.

00:21:37.705 --> 00:21:42.605
So now with that, what we're going to do
is we're going to rearrange the equation.

00:21:42.605 --> 00:21:45.915
So that way we essentially
get everything by itself.

00:21:46.265 --> 00:21:50.825
Um, we, you could kind of plug everything
into it right off the bat if you really

00:21:50.834 --> 00:21:55.515
chose to, but it's usually whenever
you're doing this problem, it's sometimes

00:21:55.515 --> 00:21:58.995
helpful to just kind of rearrange it
so you can kind of see how to use it.

00:21:59.445 --> 00:22:01.415
So when you're rearranging this.

00:22:01.865 --> 00:22:06.504
One thing that you would do is we'd
have 680 on the left hand side, and

00:22:06.504 --> 00:22:12.305
then we would want to essentially
multiply both sides by the denominator.

00:22:12.305 --> 00:22:17.064
So if I were to do that, where I
multiply the right hand side by the

00:22:17.065 --> 00:22:21.465
denominator, so that way they'll
cancel, we'll multiply the 680 as

00:22:21.465 --> 00:22:23.404
well, so that way it gets rearranged.

00:22:24.135 --> 00:22:29.504
And as a result, you'll see that the
bottom cancels, and then what that's

00:22:29.524 --> 00:22:33.625
all going to be equal to is SO3 squared.

00:22:33.994 --> 00:22:38.864
So we almost have SO3 by itself, so what's
the last little thing that you have to do?

00:22:39.125 --> 00:22:40.264
How do you remove a square?

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:43.530
Well, the opposite of a square
is a square root, right?

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:47.710
So what we're going to do is we will
square both, square root both sides.

00:22:48.730 --> 00:22:53.029
And what you can see is this formula
that we kind of came with, came up

00:22:53.029 --> 00:23:00.420
with on the right hand side is going
to be equal to SO3 as a concentration.

00:23:01.210 --> 00:23:04.640
So now what we are going to do is we're
going to plug all of our numbers in.

00:23:05.220 --> 00:23:10.720
So what we had, right, we had 680,
then our concentration of SO2.

00:23:11.130 --> 00:23:12.440
was 0.

00:23:12.580 --> 00:23:18.300
300, we'll square that, and
then times oxygen which was 0.

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:21.030
150 molar.

00:23:21.710 --> 00:23:26.250
So when we plug all those values
in, what we are going to get as

00:23:26.250 --> 00:23:30.790
a final answer is going to be 3.

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:37.340
0298 molar, and then we would round
that to 3 sig figs which would be 3.

00:23:37.340 --> 00:23:37.444
03.

00:23:37.735 --> 00:23:38.105
molar.

00:23:39.655 --> 00:23:42.344
So that's how you would
solve for a concentration.

00:23:42.574 --> 00:23:46.304
I'd recommend trying it on your own just
so you can try to rearrange it yourself.

00:23:46.574 --> 00:23:49.004
It's all, you know, it takes
a little bit of algebra, but

00:23:49.004 --> 00:23:50.364
sometimes you will have to do this.

00:23:50.364 --> 00:23:54.954
This is a very common thing to have to
do when it comes to pH problems, because

00:23:54.955 --> 00:23:59.024
you're usually solving for maybe the
hydronium or the hydrogen concentration.

00:23:59.375 --> 00:24:04.985
Or the hydroxide concentration, which
you could then convert to pH or POH and

00:24:04.985 --> 00:24:06.425
all those different species as well.

00:24:07.625 --> 00:24:09.305
Let's take a look at our next example.

00:24:09.395 --> 00:24:13.674
So for the next one, which is all about
solubility products, and I ask you write

00:24:13.755 --> 00:24:16.565
the net ionic equation, then the KSP.

00:24:16.955 --> 00:24:20.675
So if you have to define a
solubility product, it's all about

00:24:20.685 --> 00:24:22.965
how does it dissolve in solution.

00:24:23.470 --> 00:24:27.580
So the first thing is, well,
defining what is led to iodide.

00:24:27.920 --> 00:24:32.060
So when you see the Roman numerals, the
first thing for you to get out of your

00:24:32.060 --> 00:24:34.810
head is thinking that this is a subscript.

00:24:34.810 --> 00:24:38.909
A lot of students that I see think
that that is a subscript, but that's

00:24:38.909 --> 00:24:41.300
telling you what the variable charge is.

00:24:41.720 --> 00:24:47.790
So being that is led next to an iodine,
we will swap drop, um, so that that

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:50.720
charge of two will get traded down.

00:24:51.265 --> 00:24:55.045
And then that one, that one is going
to get traded down on the other side.

00:24:55.525 --> 00:25:01.345
So what we will end up having is
lead I2 as our final structure.

00:25:02.085 --> 00:25:05.794
And what we are going to be curious
about is, well, how is that going to

00:25:05.794 --> 00:25:08.615
dissolve or if it can even dissolve?

00:25:09.105 --> 00:25:12.085
One thing is that you'll
notice is that lead iodide is

00:25:12.085 --> 00:25:14.185
actually very, very insoluble.

00:25:14.405 --> 00:25:19.995
It's generally considered to be a
solid, but it can still, even though

00:25:19.995 --> 00:25:24.995
something is labeled as insoluble, it
still can break down into its ions.

00:25:25.365 --> 00:25:28.765
It just usually has a
very, very small K value.

00:25:29.269 --> 00:25:33.529
So the way that we would represent the
dissociation equation is we would write

00:25:33.529 --> 00:25:40.039
lead 2 and then we would reset the
ions So being that we have I2, we don't

00:25:40.039 --> 00:25:43.850
just write I2 like this That's a no no.

00:25:44.240 --> 00:25:49.570
The 2 would actually get brought in front
because it's like taking lead iodine and

00:25:49.570 --> 00:25:51.740
then Smashing it into a million pieces.

00:25:51.920 --> 00:25:53.800
So what exactly would
happen when you break it?

00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:59.440
It's gonna go into all of its ions So
being that these are both in solution,

00:25:59.540 --> 00:26:01.680
they are considered to be aqueous.

00:26:02.379 --> 00:26:05.170
So what we would do is our
products over reactants.

00:26:05.640 --> 00:26:12.699
So you will quickly see that the KSP is
only equal to the products because our

00:26:12.699 --> 00:26:16.610
reactant is insoluble in this situation.

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:19.639
So we would write it just like this.

00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:23.870
Now the thing is, how do you
interpret these different K values?

00:26:24.330 --> 00:26:27.840
What they're telling you is
how soluble something is.

00:26:28.344 --> 00:26:31.554
So the bigger the K
value, the more soluble.

00:26:31.975 --> 00:26:36.135
So let's say if I had the KSP for a
bunch of different, different salts.

00:26:36.145 --> 00:26:40.565
So I have lead sulfate, aluminum
phosphate, silver chloride, and

00:26:40.565 --> 00:26:42.735
then I have their respective Ks.

00:26:43.115 --> 00:26:44.224
So how do we choose?

00:26:44.655 --> 00:26:49.315
Well, whichever one is the biggest
would be your most soluble.

00:26:49.635 --> 00:26:53.055
So when you kind of look through
these, well, you see lead sulfate has

00:26:53.055 --> 00:26:55.005
the biggest number out of all these.

00:26:55.405 --> 00:26:57.585
So this would be your most soluble.

00:26:58.044 --> 00:27:01.455
Whereas aluminum phosphate would
be your least soluble because

00:27:01.455 --> 00:27:02.764
it's your tiniest number.

00:27:03.215 --> 00:27:06.774
So this would be your answer
here, right, is this top choice.

00:27:07.165 --> 00:27:08.975
Let's go on to our next example.

00:27:09.185 --> 00:27:12.414
So for our last example, what we
are going to be doing is writing

00:27:12.414 --> 00:27:14.414
the Ka for the following acid.

00:27:14.794 --> 00:27:19.165
So, it's very common that you will
have to write Ka expressions or even

00:27:19.235 --> 00:27:21.965
Kb expressions for general chemistry.

00:27:22.384 --> 00:27:25.514
So one of the things that we
will do is when we are writing

00:27:25.514 --> 00:27:29.534
these, we typically are writing
how they would react with water.

00:27:29.854 --> 00:27:34.665
So if I have HIO3 and it's reacting
with water, well, the first thing

00:27:34.665 --> 00:27:38.425
is, is you have to label the acid
and the base because that determines

00:27:38.455 --> 00:27:41.075
how they are going to behave, right?

00:27:41.195 --> 00:27:43.544
Acids donate protons.

00:27:44.010 --> 00:27:49.740
So what's gonna happen is the H from the
I Otic acid is gonna donate to water.

00:27:50.320 --> 00:27:56.620
So when we write that, we are
going to write our IO three minus

00:27:57.340 --> 00:28:03.570
and then our H three oh plus
for our other expression, right?

00:28:04.080 --> 00:28:06.090
Or sorry for our other product.

00:28:06.670 --> 00:28:10.960
So now that we have that, what
we are gonna now do is write RKA,

00:28:11.200 --> 00:28:13.450
which is products over reactants.

00:28:14.014 --> 00:28:19.145
So being that the only thing here is that
that we don't include is water because

00:28:19.145 --> 00:28:25.214
that is a liquid The rest of these would
all be considered aqueous Yet they're ions

00:28:25.214 --> 00:28:27.035
and that they're soluble in a mixture.

00:28:27.545 --> 00:28:33.815
So we would write our products all
over our reactants Okay, so we have

00:28:33.865 --> 00:28:42.690
io3 minus HO3 plus All over H, I,
O, 3, that's our final piece, okay?

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:44.340
And that's how we'd write Ka and Kb.

00:28:44.850 --> 00:28:46.990
Now the last thing is, sometimes
you have to interpret the

00:28:46.990 --> 00:28:48.470
strength of an acid or base.

00:28:48.950 --> 00:28:53.210
So depending on what the size of your
Ka value is, determines the strength.

00:28:53.300 --> 00:28:54.900
Just like we did with Ksp.

00:28:55.360 --> 00:29:01.050
The larger the number, the more ions
that are being generated in the solution.

00:29:01.410 --> 00:29:04.000
The more ions, the stronger it is.

00:29:04.270 --> 00:29:07.739
So the stronger the electrolyte, the
stronger the acid, the stronger the base.

00:29:08.295 --> 00:29:11.225
And you can also do equilibrium
problems with these two where it comes

00:29:11.225 --> 00:29:15.585
to Le Chatelier's principle, um, or
just determining how acids and bases

00:29:15.585 --> 00:29:17.905
behave with figuring out pH or pOH.

00:29:18.375 --> 00:29:22.485
All right, so this has been my summary of
all the major problems that you generally

00:29:22.485 --> 00:29:23.894
will see in terms of equilibrium.

